  "image_name": "9a0351677d8e63154d2f9ab3bcdfc792.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two energy-level pictures for a donor–acceptor interface in an organic semiconductor system, emphasizing how the interface changes the electronic states.\n\nOn the left, the donor and acceptor each show broad polaron levels, with the donor levels drawn on one side and the acceptor levels on the other. Between them is an explicit interlayer containing a series of opposite charges, which creates an interfacial energy offset labeled as a dipole-related shift. This suggests that a built-in electrostatic dipole at the interface raises the relative energy on the acceptor side compared with the donor side, while the Fermi level remains roughly centered.\n\nOn the right, the same donor and acceptor polaron levels are shown without a drawn physical interlayer, but new charge-transfer states appear near the Fermi level: a positive ICT state on the donor and a negative ICT state on the acceptor. The energy shift is now labeled as an ICT-related offset. Visually, the message is that interfacial charge transfer can generate the energy step between donor and acceptor, offering an alternative but related explanation to a simple interfacial dipole layer.",
